course: dictionary
lesson: 10716
title: Unusual
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unusual means not usual or common. It is different from what normally happens or what you normally see. «It is unusual for it to snow in July.» Snow in July is different from what normally happens, so unusual is the right word.
explain: Unusual can also mean interesting because it is different. «She wore an unusual hat with a feather on top.» Most hats do not have feathers, so people notice it. Ask yourself: is this different from normal? If yes, unusual probably fits.
explain: Unusual can be a warning sign too. In safety lessons, an unusual smell, sound or behaviour, like a gas smell or a stranger asking odd questions, means you should tell an adult. «Nothing unusual» means everything looks normal.
fact: Unusual is an adjective: unusual, more unusual, most unusual.
fact: «Unusual» means not usual or common, or different from what normally happens.
fact: «Unusual» can also mean interesting because it is different, like «an unusual hat».
fact: In science, an unusual result should be checked and repeated.
fact: Useful phrases: something unusual, unusual for, it's unusual to, nothing unusual.
exercise: «It is unusual for the shop to be closed on a Sunday.» What does unusual mean here? | *Not normal, different from what usually happens | Very large | Very happy | Kind and friendly
exercise: In science, Ruby gets an unusual result. What should she do? | Hide the results in her bag | Throw the results away straight away | Tell the whole class it is wrong without checking | *Check it and repeat the experiment
exercise: Jake smells an unusual smoke coming from the kitchen. What should he do? | Ignore it, because it is probably nothing | Wave the smoke away with a book | *Tell an adult straight away | Go back to bed
test: «The sky was an unusual shade of green before the storm.» What does unusual suggest? | Hot and sunny | *Different from the normal sky | Ordinary and expected | Dark and rainy only
test: Which phrase means everything looks normal? | Something unusual | Unusually loud | *Nothing unusual | Most unusual of all
exam: Which sentence uses unusual to mean interesting because it is different? | We always eat our lunch at twelve. | The class was quiet, as usual. | *She painted the house an unusual shade of purple. | The bus is late again, as usual.
